    {
      "type": "text",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Freshness and validation are separate cache decisions"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Cache-Control sets freshness policy: max-age and s-maxage define reuse windows, while public and private determine who may store a response.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "no-cache allows storage but requires validation before reuse; no-store prevents storage; and must-revalidate prohibits serving a stale response.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "ETag identifies a representation, allowing a client or intermediary to send If-None-Match and receive 304 Not Modified when it is unchanged.",
          "bullet": false
        },
        {
          "text": "A 304 reuses the cached body and updates applicable metadata; it saves bandwidth but does not make content fresh indefinitely.",
          "bullet": false
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Separate freshness directives from representation validation: Cache-Control governs reuse and storage, while ETag enables conditional checks. Emphasize that 304 reuses an existing body without creating indefinite freshness."
}

